      "source": [
        "## 10. Findings\n",
        "\n",
        "**Answer:** on Monday 13 October 2031, plan for about **19 beds** for emergency admissions between 12:00 and 20:00. There is a 90% chance that 24 or fewer are needed. This assumes the ED meets its target (80% admitted within 4 hours, 99% within 12).\n",
        "\n",
        "What the experiments below showed:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **The answer is robust.** Missing the 4-hour target by 10 points, or switching to a Saturday, moves it by less than one bed.\n",
        "- **Model choices matter more for calibration than for the headline number.** A small grid search and sigmoid calibration lowered test log loss at every snapshot time. Neither the triage ablation nor a shorter training window helped, so both were reverted.\n",
        "- **The in-ED predictions are slightly conservative.** Across 92 test days, 12:00 over-predicts by 0.2 admissions a day, and the 90% intervals cover 97–99% of days.\n",
        "\n",
        "Open questions: weekday-specific arrival rates for the yet-to-arrive model, and whether the over-wide intervals can be tightened without losing coverage."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "notes",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Research notes\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Target 80% → 70% within 4 h:** expected beds 19.8 → 19.1, 90th percentile 25 → 24. A slower ED delays some arrivals past 20:00, but the effect is under one bed. The answer is not very sensitive to the target.\n",
        "- **Saturday 11 Oct → Monday 13 Oct:** expected beds 19.1 → 19.0, observed admissions from the ED 7 → 6. The yet-to-arrive model is fitted on all days pooled, so only the in-ED component can change with the weekday. It barely moves here. *Todo: fit the arrival rates separately for weekdays and weekends?*\n",
        "- **Grid search over `n_estimators` ∈ {30, 100} × `max_depth` ∈ {3, 5}:** the 06:00 model improves a lot (log loss 0.195 → 0.063, AUC 0.987 → 0.997), 15:30 improves too (0.056 → 0.039), but 09:30 gets worse (0.074 → 0.108). Expected beds 19.0 → 18.6. Keeping it, but the 09:30 regression needs a look.\n",
        "- **Isotonic → sigmoid calibration:** better at every snapshot time (log loss now 0.030–0.047, and the 09:30 regression is gone). On EPUDD, isotonic gave step-shaped predicted curves at 06:00 and 09:30 because many patients shared the same probability; sigmoid is smooth and tracks the observed counts more closely. At 12:00 the observed points sit above the predicted ones under both methods: we slightly over-predict. Expected beds 18.6 → 18.4.\n",
        "- **Ablation: drop Manchester triage acuity:** log loss slightly worse at four of five times (06:00 0.047 → 0.056), expected beds unchanged at 18.4. Little unique signal; presumably elapsed time and consultation data already carry it. **Reverted:** it costs accuracy and gains nothing, and clinicians will expect triage in the model.\n",
        "- **Train from May instead of March (42,024 → 25,226 snapshots):** log loss worse at every time (09:30 0.033 → 0.055, 22:00 0.035 → 0.041). Expected beds 18.4 → 18.5. The yet-to-arrive model is unaffected: it uses the `training_validation_test` column in `inpatient_arrivals.csv`, not these dates. **Reverted** to March: more history is better here.\n",
        "- **Across all 92 test days (new bias table):** 12:00 over-predicts by 0.20 admissions a day (7.64 vs 7.43, about 3%); every other time is within 0.1. The 90% intervals contain the observed count on 97–99% of days, so the intervals are conservative: too wide, but safe for bed planning."
      ]
